{% sec 1, "Markets Rhyme", "Why study old crashes?", "Markets rhyme" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Picture a row of dominoes. The first one can be knocked by anything: a gust of wind, a cat, a sneeze. You can't predict which. But you <em>can</em> see how close together the dominoes are standing. If they're tightly packed, any small push knocks them all over; if they're spaced out, one falls and the rest stand.</p>
    <p>Crashes work the same way. The trigger is always different: a war, a default, a software bug, a surprise from a central bank. But almost every crash grows the same way:</p>
  </div>
  <ul class="facts">
    <li><span class="n">1</span><span>A shock.<span class="why">Something surprises the market and prices drop.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">2</span><span>Leveraged holders are forced to sell.<span class="why">Margin calls, stop-losses and risk limits trigger: the cascade from Margin and Leverage.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">3</span><span>The selling pushes prices further from fair value.<span class="why">Forced sellers don't care about price, and buyers step back.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">4</span><span>Lower prices force the next group to sell.<span class="why">The spiral feeds itself until the leverage is gone, or someone steps in: often a central bank acting as {% gl "lender of last resort" %}lender of last resort{% endgl %}.</span></span></li>
  </ul>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>How bad it gets depends on three things, the spacing of the dominoes:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Leverage:</strong> more borrowing means more forced sellers.</li>
      <li><strong>Crowding:</strong> if everyone holds the same trade, everyone runs for the same door, and trouble in one place spreads to others ({% gl "contagion" %}contagion{% endgl %}).</li>
      <li><strong>Liquidity:</strong> how much buying is there to absorb the selling? It tends to vanish exactly when it's needed, because market makers widen spreads and step back when things get wild.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>None of those three is a secret. Margin debt, short interest, credit spreads and the price of options all show how fragile a market is <em>before</em> anything happens. You can't predict the trigger, but you can measure the fragility.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="big-idea">
    <span class="lbl">The one thing to remember</span>
    <p>The shock is random; the crash isn't. Leverage, crowding and thin liquidity turn a small push into a fall.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

