{% sec 4, "Five Lessons That Keep Repeating", "What should I take from all this as a trader?", "Five lessons" %}
  <ul class="facts">
    <li><span class="n">1</span><span>Leverage makes losses bigger than the fundamentals would.<span class="why">The asset falls \(20\%\); the leveraged holder loses everything. Every crash on the list has borrowing at its centre.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">2</span><span>In a crisis, correlations go to one.<span class="why">Assets that move independently in calm markets fall together when everyone sells whatever they can. Diversification fails exactly when you need it (section 8 shows the maths).</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">3</span><span>Liquidity disappears when it's most needed.<span class="why">You can't sell what nobody wants to buy. A stop-loss is a request to sell, not a guarantee of a price.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">4</span><span>"Once in a century" happens every decade.<span class="why">Models built on the normal distribution say huge moves are almost impossible. Real markets have {% gl "fat tails" %}fat tails{% endgl %}: Black Monday was a move of roughly \(20\) standard deviations, which a normal model says should never happen in the lifetime of the universe.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">5</span><span>People react the same way every time.<span class="why">Denial, then hope, then panic, then giving up ({% gl "capitulation" %}capitulation{% endgl %}). Recognising the stage you're in is half the battle.</span></span></li>
  </ul>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>For the blog's Reading list series, Deltashark's picks here are <em>When Genius Failed</em> by Roger Lowenstein (LTCM), <em>Flash Boys</em> by Michael Lewis (high-frequency trading), <em>Dark Pools</em> by Scott Patterson, Larry Harris's textbook <em>Trading and Exchanges</em>, Ed Thorp's <em>A Man for All Markets</em>, and Gregory Zuckerman's <em>The Man Who Solved the Market</em> (Renaissance).</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 5, "And in FX, Gold and Bonds?", "What were the big crashes in my three markets?", "FX, gold and bonds" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <h3>FX: when a currency peg breaks</h3>
    <p>FX crises usually start when a country tries to hold its currency at a fixed level and the market decides it can't. On <strong>Black Wednesday</strong>, 16 September 1992, the UK spent billions of pounds and raised interest rates to defend sterling's place in Europe's exchange rate mechanism, then gave up the same day; George Soros's fund was famously on the other side. The 1997 Asian crisis followed the same script across Thailand, Indonesia and South Korea. In 2015 the Swiss central bank did the opposite, suddenly dropping a cap on its currency, and the franc jumped about \(30\%\) in minutes (Prime Brokerage covers what that did to FX brokers). The lesson for an FX trader: a currency held still by a central bank isn't low-risk; it's a big move waiting to happen.</p>
    <h3>Gold and silver: squeezes and margin rises</h3>
    <p>In 1979–80 the Hunt brothers tried to corner the silver market, pushing the price up roughly eightfold with borrowed money. COMEX changed its rules to allow only selling, margins rose, and on "Silver Thursday", 27 March 1980, the price collapsed. Silver did it again in 2011, when five margin rises in about nine days helped knock a quarter off the price in a week. On 15 April 2013, gold fell around \(9\%\) in a day, its biggest drop in about 30 years, as heavy selling of futures hit a crowded long market.</p>
    <h3>Bonds: the "safe" market that keeps breaking</h3>
    <p>In the 1994 "bond massacre", surprise US rate rises sent yields sharply higher and losses hit leveraged bond holders worldwide, including Orange County in California, which went bankrupt. In March 2020, even US Treasuries, the world's safest asset, stopped trading smoothly as everyone sold them for cash at once, until the Fed bought them in huge amounts. And in September 2022, UK gilts fell so fast after the mini-budget that leveraged pension funds faced a wave of collateral calls (Margin and Leverage tells the story). Since then, LDI funds have had to hold enough buffer to survive yields rising about \(2.5\) percentage points.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 6, "Key Words", "What does the desk call these things?" %}
  <div class="terms">
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Circuit breaker</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>An automatic pause in trading when prices fall too far, too fast.</p><p class="eg">"We hit the 7% circuit breaker, trading's halted."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>LULD</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Limit Up-Limit Down: price bands that pause a single US stock that moves too quickly.</p><p class="eg">"It's in a LULD halt, wait five minutes."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Kill switch</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>A button or automatic limit that stops all of a firm's trading at once.</p><p class="eg">"Hit the kill switch, the algo's gone wild."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Contagion</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>A problem in one market or firm spreading to others.</p><p class="eg">"The worry is contagion into the other banks."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Fire sale</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>Forced selling at whatever price is available.</p><p class="eg">"Someone's having a fire sale in credit."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Tail risk</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>The risk of rare, extreme moves that normal models say are almost impossible.</p><p class="eg">"Buy some puts against the tail risk."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Capitulation</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>The moment holders give up and sell at any price, often near the bottom.</p><p class="eg">"Volume's huge, that looks like capitulation."</p></div>
    <div class="term"><div class="term-top"><h3>Lender of last resort</h3><span class="tag desk">Desk</span></div><p>A central bank lending or buying when nobody else will, to stop a panic.</p><p class="eg">"The Fed's back as lender of last resort."</p></div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 7, "How does this link to Trading, Real Estate and Fintech?", "Where the crash lessons turn up in each part of the plan.", "How it links to the plan" %}
  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Trading: build your own circuit breakers</h3><a class="theme-link tone-forest" href="/hub/trading/">Trading</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>Every lesson here applies to one trader. Keep leverage low enough to survive a move far bigger than you expect. Don't crowd into the same trade everyone's talking about. Remember that a stop can gap straight past its level when liquidity vanishes. And set your own kill switch: a daily loss limit that ends the day, no arguing. Prop firms enforce exactly this, which is why their rules look like a regulator's.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Real estate: most big crashes start or end in property</h3><a class="theme-link tone-burgundy" href="/hub/real-estate/">Real Estate</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The 2008 crisis started with US mortgages; the UK's early-1990s crash left a generation in negative equity; China's Evergrande, the world's most indebted developer, defaulted in 2021 after years of borrowing to build. Property is slow, leveraged and hard to sell quickly: the three crash amplifiers in one asset. For a development business, that means modest borrowing, enough cash to survive a year with no sales, and lenders who won't panic (the Prime Brokerage lesson again).</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="link-block">
    <div class="link-block-head"><h3>Fintech and Nigeria: crashes that reshaped the system</h3><a class="theme-link tone-navy" href="/hub/fintech/">Fintech and Nigeria</a></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>After the 2008–09 margin-loan crash, the CBN removed the bosses of eight banks in 2009 and AMCON was created in 2010 to buy their bad loans. The FX crises of 2016 and 2022–23, when dollars were scarce and foreign investors' money was stuck, ended with the CBN unifying the official exchange rates in June 2023 and the naira falling sharply. Each crisis rewrote the rules, and each rewrite opened space for new products: better FX access, transparent pricing, tools that help people manage leverage and currency risk.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 8, "The Maths Underneath", "How do you put numbers on a crash?" %}
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Volatility of an equal-weight portfolio of \(n\) assets (each volatility \(\sigma\), correlation \(\rho\))</span>\[ \sigma_p = \sigma \sqrt{\frac{1}{n} + \left(1 - \frac{1}{n}\right)\rho} \]</div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">Gain needed to recover from a drawdown \(d\)</span>\[ \text{gain} = \frac{d}{1 - d} \qquad 50\% \text{ fall} \to 100\% \text{ gain needed} \]</div>
  <div class="formula"><span class="lbl">How many standard deviations a move is</span>\[ z = \frac{\text{move}}{\text{daily volatility}} \]</div>

  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example 1</span>
    <h3>Ten assets, each with \(20\%\) volatility. What happens to diversification as correlation rises?</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Calm, \(\rho = 0.2\): \(\sigma_p = 20\% \times \sqrt{0.1 + 0.9 \times 0.2} = 20\% \times \sqrt{0.28} \approx 10.6\%\).</li>
      <li>Crisis, \(\rho = 0.9\): \(\sigma_p = 20\% \times \sqrt{0.1 + 0.9 \times 0.9} = 20\% \times \sqrt{0.91} \approx 19.1\%\).</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer">Diversification halved the risk in calm markets, but barely helps in a crisis.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="worked">
    <span class="lbl">Worked example 2</span>
    <h3>On Black Monday the Dow fell \(22.6\%\). Its typical daily move was about \(1\%\).</h3>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>\(z \approx \frac{22.6\%}{1\%} \approx 22\) standard deviations.</li>
      <li>Under a normal distribution, even a \(5\)-standard-deviation day should happen about once in \(14{,}000\) years.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="answer">Market returns have much fatter tails than the normal distribution. The Probability topic's Fat Tails note picks this up.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 9, "Practice", "Have a go, then open the answer." %}
  <div class="practice">
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">1</span><span>Describe, in four steps, how a small shock becomes a crash.</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>A shock drops prices; leveraged holders hit margin calls and limits and are forced to sell; their selling pushes prices further; lower prices force the next group to sell, and the spiral continues until the leverage is gone or someone steps in.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">2</span><span>Five assets each have \(30\%\) volatility. What's the portfolio volatility at a correlation of \(0\), and at \(1\)?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>At \(\rho = 0\): \(30\% \times \sqrt{0.2} \approx 13.4\%\). At \(\rho = 1\): \(30\%\), no diversification at all.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">3</span><span>A fund falls \(40\%\). What gain does it need to get back to where it started?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>\(\frac{0.4}{0.6} \approx 66.7\%\).</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">4</span><span>Match the crash to the rule: Black Monday 1987, Flash Crash 2010, the 2008 crisis, GameStop 2021.</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>Black Monday: market-wide circuit breakers. Flash Crash: Limit Up-Limit Down and single-stock pauses. 2008: Dodd-Frank, the Volcker Rule, Basel III, central clearing. GameStop: part of the push for T+1 settlement.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">5</span><span>What lesson did Knight Capital teach every trading firm?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>That operational risk can be as deadly as market risk. A faulty software release made Knight's algorithms send millions of wrong orders, losing \(\$440\) million in about 45 minutes. Firms now rely on careful release procedures, pre-trade risk limits and kill switches.</span></div></details></div>
    <div class="pq"><p class="pq-text"><span class="n">6</span><span>Interview question: which signs would tell you a market is fragile before it crashes?</span></p>
      <details><summary>Show answer</summary><div><span>High and rising leverage (margin debt, repo borrowing), crowded positioning (short interest, everyone in the same trade), thin liquidity (wide spreads, shallow order books) and stretched valuations, plus warning lights such as widening credit spreads and options prices showing demand for crash protection. You can't predict the trigger, but you can see the fragility.</span></div></details></div>
  </div>
{% endsec %}
